---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Diagram from '../components/Diagram.astro';
const faq = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'FAQPage',
  mainEntity: [
    { '@type': 'Question', name: 'What does DNSSEC protect against?', acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: 'DNSSEC protects against tampered DNS answers: cache poisoning and on-path spoofing that redirect users to attacker-controlled servers. It does not encrypt DNS queries; that is DoH or DoT.' } },
    { '@type': 'Question', name: 'Should I enable DNSSEC?', acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: 'Yes if your DNS host and registrar integrate it cleanly (Cloudflare, Route 53, and major registrars do). The risk window is key rotation handled out of order.' } },
    { '@type': 'Question', name: 'Why does DNSSEC break my whole domain?', acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: 'Validating resolvers must refuse answers that fail verification. A stale DS record after key rotation breaks the chain of trust, so the entire domain returns SERVFAIL.' } },
  ],
};
---
<BaseLayout title="What Is DNSSEC and Should You Enable It?" description="DNSSEC explained: how signed zones and the chain of trust stop cache poisoning, the DS/DNSKEY relationship, and safe activation and rollover." schema={[faq]} breadcrumb={[["Home", "/"], ["DNS Guide", "/dns/"], ["What Is DNSSEC"]]}>
  <div class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-4 py-12">
    <nav class="mb-4 text-sm text-ink-faint" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
      <a href="/" class="hover:text-accent">Home</a>
       <span aria-hidden="true">/</span>
      <a href="/dns/" class="hover:text-accent">DNS Guide</a>
       <span aria-hidden="true">/</span>
      <span class="text-ink-dim">What Is DNSSEC</span>
    </nav>
    <h1 class="font-display text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight text-ink sm:text-4xl">What Is DNSSEC?</h1>
    <div class="pd-answer" data-vs="answer">
      <p class="text-lg leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">
      DNSSEC (DNS Security Extensions) adds cryptographic signatures to DNS answers, so a resolver
      can prove the answer it received is the one your zone actually published. Without it, DNS is
      trusted on vibes: whoever answers first wins.
    </p>
    </div>

    <h2 class="font-display mt-10 text-xl font-semibold text-ink">The 4 core DNSSEC record types (RFC 4034)</h2>
    <section class="not-prose my-4" aria-label="Reference table">
      <div class="glass rounded-2xl p-5 shadow-lg sm:p-7 overflow-x-auto">
        <table class="pdns-table" style="width:100%">
          <thead><tr><th>Record</th><th>Role</th><th>Key detail</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody><tr><td class="font-medium text-ink">RRSIG</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">Digital signature over each record set</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">Must be refreshed before signature expiry</td></tr><tr><td class="font-medium text-ink">DNSKEY</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">Zone signing key (flag 256) and key signing key (flag 257)</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">The KSK signs the DNSKEY set</td></tr><tr><td class="font-medium text-ink">DS</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">Hash of the KSK published in the parent zone</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">The chain of trust link at the registrar</td></tr><tr><td class="font-medium text-ink">NSEC / NSEC3</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">Authenticated denial of existence</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">Proves a name genuinely does not exist</td></tr></tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </section>

    <h2 class="font-display mt-10 text-xl font-semibold text-ink">DNSSEC validation states in resolvers</h2>
    <section class="not-prose my-4" aria-label="Reference table">
      <div class="glass rounded-2xl p-5 shadow-lg sm:p-7 overflow-x-auto">
        <table class="pdns-table" style="width:100%">
          <thead><tr><th>State</th><th>Meaning</th><th>What you see</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody><tr><td class="font-medium text-ink">Secure</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">Chain of trust validates to the root</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">AD flag set on the answer</td></tr><tr><td class="font-medium text-ink">Insecure</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">Zone is unsigned; no DS at the parent</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">Normal answer, no AD flag</td></tr><tr><td class="font-medium text-ink">Bogus</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">Broken chain: mismatched DS, expired RRSIG</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">SERVFAIL for the whole zone</td></tr></tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </section>
<nav class="pd-toc" aria-label="On this page">
      <p class="font-mono text-[10px] uppercase tracking-[0.18em] text-accent">On this page</p>
      <ul>
        <li><a href="#the-threat-it-stops">The threat it stops</a></li>
        <li><a href="#how-the-chain-of-trust-works">How the chain of trust works</a></li>
        <li><a href="#what-dnssec-does-not-do">What DNSSEC does not do</a></li>
        <li><a href="#enable-it-safely">Enable it safely</a></li>
        <li><a href="#check-your-domain-right-now">Check your domain right now</a></li>
        <li><a href="#frequently-asked-questions">Frequently asked questions</a></li>
        <li><a href="#related">Related</a></li>
      </ul>
    </nav>
    <Diagram name="dnssec-chain" caption="The DNSSEC chain of trust: root to TLD to your domain" />

    <div class="rich-text mt-8">
      <h2 id="the-threat-it-stops">The threat it stops</h2>
      <p>
        Classic DNS responses are unauthenticated plaintext. An attacker positioned on the network, or
        one who has poisoned a resolver's cache, can forge an answer that points your domain at their
        server. Users type your URL, see your site load (from their fake copy), and hand over
        credentials. DNSSEC closes exactly this hole: a forged answer cannot carry a valid signature,
        so validating resolvers reject it instead of serving it.
      </p>

      <h2 id="how-the-chain-of-trust-works">How the chain of trust works</h2>
      <p>
        Your zone signs its records with private keys and publishes the public halves as
        <a href="/dnskey-record/">DNSKEY records</a>. Self-published keys prove nothing on their own,
        so the parent zone publishes a <a href="/ds-record/">DS record</a>: a digest of your key-signing
        key. Validators walk root → TLD → your domain, checking each link against the DS above it.
        Every link verifies, and your answers are provably yours. Any link breaks, and the resolver
        must refuse the whole domain, which is why DNSSEC misconfiguration produces total outages
        rather than partial ones.
      </p>

      <h2 id="what-dnssec-does-not-do">What DNSSEC does not do</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>It does not encrypt queries; observers still see domains being resolved. Encryption is <a href="/doh-vs-dot/">DoH/DoT</a>.</li>
        <li>It does not stop DDoS attacks against your nameservers.</li>
        <li>It authenticates answers, not the content those answers lead to; TLS still does that job.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 id="enable-it-safely">Enable it safely</h2>
      <ol class="list-decimal space-y-1.5 pl-5">
        <li>Turn on DNSSEC at your DNS host (a toggle on Cloudflare and Route 53).</li>
        <li>Copy the DS record it shows you to your registrar, all four fields exactly.</li>
        <li>Wait for propagation, then verify: our record lookup with DS selected should return your record, and the domain should resolve normally through Quad9 or Cloudflare (validating resolvers).</li>
        <li>Let your provider handle key rotation. If rotating manually: publish the new key's DS, wait, then retire the old key. Never the reverse order.</li>
      </ol>

      <h2 id="check-your-domain-right-now">Check your domain right now</h2>
      <p>
        Run the homepage <a href="/">security scanner</a>: the DNSSEC check looks for your DS record and
        reports whether the zone is signed, alongside SPF, DKIM, DMARC, and AI readiness.
      </p>

      

      <h2 class="font-display mt-10 text-xl font-semibold text-ink" id="frequently-asked-questions">Frequently asked questions</h2>
      <div class="mt-3 grid gap-3">
        <details class="group rounded-xl border border-line bg-panel/60 px-5 py-4 open:border-accent/40"><summary class="flex cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-4 font-semibold text-ink [&\::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">What does DNSSEC protect against?<span class="text-accent transition-transform duration-200 group-open:rotate-45">+</span></summary><p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">DNSSEC protects against tampered DNS answers: cache poisoning and on-path spoofing that redirect users to attacker-controlled servers. It does not encrypt DNS queries; that is DoH or DoT.</p></details>
        <details class="group rounded-xl border border-line bg-panel/60 px-5 py-4 open:border-accent/40"><summary class="flex cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-4 font-semibold text-ink [&\::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">Should I enable DNSSEC?<span class="text-accent transition-transform duration-200 group-open:rotate-45">+</span></summary><p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">Yes if your DNS host and registrar integrate it cleanly (Cloudflare, Route 53, and major registrars do). The risk window is key rotation handled out of order.</p></details>
        <details class="group rounded-xl border border-line bg-panel/60 px-5 py-4 open:border-accent/40"><summary class="flex cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-4 font-semibold text-ink [&\::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">Why does DNSSEC break my whole domain?<span class="text-accent transition-transform duration-200 group-open:rotate-45">+</span></summary><p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">Validating resolvers must refuse answers that fail verification. A stale DS record after key rotation breaks the chain of trust, so the entire domain returns SERVFAIL.</p></details>
      </div>      <h2 id="related">Related</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><a href="/ds-record/">DS records</a> and <a href="/dnskey-record/">DNSKEY records</a></li>
        <li><a href="/enable-dnssec-cloudflare/">Enable DNSSEC in Cloudflare</a></li>
        <li><a href="/dns-security/">DNS security cluster</a></li>
        <li><a href="/dns-cache-poisoning/">Cache poisoning</a></li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
